description:
A pack folder's `pack.toml`: what the loader reads before it runs the
pack, as a mod's `[package.metadata.enshwick]` says it for a DLL.

# Examples

```
use enshwick::packs::{PackManifest, PackSide};

let manifest = PackManifest::parse(r#"
id = "you.lanterns"
version = "1.0.0"
name = "Lanterns"
exports = ["event:lit"]

[requires]
market = { id = "you.trading-post", version = ">=0.5, <1.0" }
"#)?;
assert_eq!(manifest.id, "you.lanterns");
assert_eq!(manifest.exports, ["event:lit"]);
assert_eq!(manifest.side, PackSide::Both);
assert_eq!(manifest.requires["market"].id, "you.trading-post");
# Ok::<(), enshwick::packs::PackError>(())
```

Mode.description:
How links act on a point's answer, as the owning mod chooses.

# Examples

```
use enshwick::points::Mode;

assert!(Mode::Override.changes());
assert!(!Mode::Observe.changes());
```

PackDependency.description:
One dependency of a pack, the value under its alias in `requires`,
`after` or `breaks`: a mod id, or a table with the `id`
and a `version` requirement.

# Examples

```
use enshwick::packs::PackManifest;

let manifest = PackManifest::parse(r#"
id = "you.lanterns"
version = "1.0.0"

[requires]
market = { id = "you.trading-post", version = ">=0.5, <1.0" }
lockers = "you.lockers"
"#)?;
assert_eq!(manifest.requires["market"].id, "you.trading-post");
assert_eq!(manifest.requires["market"].version, ">=0.5, <1.0");
assert_eq!(manifest.requires["lockers"].version, "");
# Ok::<(), enshwick::packs::PackError>(())
```

PackPoint.description:
One extension point a pack's scripts own, a `[[point]]` table of
`pack.toml`: other mods link into it, as into a Rust mod's `#[point]`.

# Examples

```
use enshwick::packs::PackManifest;
use enshwick::points::Mode;

let manifest = PackManifest::parse(r#"
id = "you.trading-post"
version = "1.0.0"
exports = ["point:price"]

[[point]]
name = "price"
mode = "override"
refusable = true
output = "u32"
input = [{ name = "item", type = "enshwick.item_id" }, { name = "count", type = "u32" }]
"#)?;
let price = &manifest.point[0];
assert_eq!(price.mode, Mode::Override);
let layout = price.layout().ok_or("no layout")?;
assert_eq!((layout.input[1].offset, layout.size), (4, 8));
# Ok::<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>>(())
```
